\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A tree has only its vacuum}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Let the spatial graph be a finite tree. The rooted-tree
 coordinates of \ref{fdip-00MT} send every configuration to
 the configuration with all links equal to the identity by a
 vertex gauge transformation. Its quotient has one point.
 Thus the [physical Hilbert space](fdip-0083) consists of
 constants and has dimension one. A pointwise gauge-invariant
 spatial potential is constant as well. The normalized positive
 vacuum is #{\Omega=1}, and every bounded physical operator
 is scalar. Its centered correlation in \ref{fdip-00N7}
 vanishes at every separation.}

\p{Backtracking closed paths on a tree have identity holonomy.
 A nonconstant traced-loop measurement requires an actual cycle;
 it cannot be supplied by choosing a different representation
 on this tree. The same reasoning applies componentwise to a
 finite forest.}
